{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 7,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.105,
            0.17,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": "CIRCLES"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.863,
            0.105,
            0.898,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": "151"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.144,
            0.715,
            0.164
          ],
          "content": "Now, $\\angle TPR  + \\angle RPO = 90^{\\circ} = \\angle TPR  + \\angle PTR$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.169,
            0.341,
            0.188
          ],
          "content": "So, $\\angle  RPO  = \\angle  PTR$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.125,
            0.195,
            0.899,
            0.215
          ],
          "content": "Therefore, right triangle TRP is similar to the right triangle PRO by AA similarity."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.241,
            0.201,
            0.26
          ],
          "content": "This gives"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.364,
            0.225,
            0.789,
            0.269
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{ TP }{ PO } = \\frac{ RP }{ RO },  i.e., \\frac{ TP }{5} = \\frac{4}{3}  or   TP  = \\frac{20}{3}  cm .",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.277,
            0.815,
            0.297
          ],
          "content": "**Note :** TP can also be found by using the Pythagoras Theorem, as follows:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.304,
            0.139,
            0.32
          ],
          "content": "Let"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.33,
            0.302,
            0.615,
            0.322
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{TP} = x \\quad  and  \\quad \\mathrm{TR} = y. \\quad \\mathrm{Then}",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.338,
            0.327,
            0.896,
            0.348
          ],
          "content": "x ^ { 2 } = y ^ { 2 } + 1 6 \\qquad \\mathrm { ( T a k i n g ~ r i g h t ~ \\Delta ~ P R T ) }\\tag{1}",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.294,
            0.353,
            0.896,
            0.373
          ],
          "content": "x ^ { 2 } + 5 ^ { 2 } = ( y + 3 ) ^ { 2 } \\qquad \\mathrm { ( T a k i n g ~ r i g h t ~ \\Delta ~ O P T ) }\\tag{2}",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.379,
            0.408,
            0.398
          ],
          "content": "Subtracting (1) from (2), we get"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.336,
            0.399,
            0.644,
            0.441
          ],
          "content": "25 = 6y - 7 \\quad  or  \\quad y = \\frac{32}{6} = \\frac{16}{3}",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.464,
            0.205,
            0.483
          ],
          "content": "Therefore,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.338,
            0.448,
            0.735,
            0.492
          ],
          "content": "x^{2}=\\left(\\frac{16}{3}\\right)^{2}+16=\\frac{16}{9}(16+9)=\\frac{16\\times25}{9}",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.793,
            0.464,
            0.897,
            0.483
          ],
          "content": "[From (1)]"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.522,
            0.125,
            0.535
          ],
          "content": "or"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.347,
            0.505,
            0.429,
            0.546
          ],
          "content": "x = \\frac{20}{3}",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_18.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.397,
            0.576,
            0.603,
            0.594
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "EXERCISE 10.2"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.605,
            0.618,
            0.623
          ],
          "content": "In Q.1 to 3, choose the correct option and give justification."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.629,
            0.9,
            0.647
          ],
          "content": ". rom a po nt Q, t e engt o t e tangent to a c rcle is 24 cm and the distance of Q from 1 F i h l h f h i"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.123,
            0.629,
            0.561,
            0.712
          ],
          "continues_prev": true,
          "content": "the centre is 25 cm. The radius of the circle is (A) 7 cm (B) 12 cm (C) 15 cm (D) 24.5 cm"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.123,
            0.716,
            0.563,
            0.817
          ],
          "content": "**2.** In Fig. 10.11, if TP and TQ are the two tangents to a circle with centre O so that $\\angle POQ = 110^{\\circ}$ then $\\angle \\mathrm{PTQ}$ is equal to (A) $6 0 ^ { \\circ }$ (B) $7 0 ^ { \\circ }$ (C) $8 0 ^ { \\circ }$ (D) $9 0 ^ { \\circ }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.623,
            0.647,
            0.813,
            0.797
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.661,
                0.802,
                0.769,
                0.819
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 10.11"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_image_24.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.822,
            0.901,
            0.905
          ],
          "content": "**3.** If tangents PA and PB from a point P to a circle with centre O are inclined to each other at angle of $8 0 ^ { \\circ }$ , then $\\angle \\mathrm { P O A }$ is equal to (A) $5 0 ^ { \\circ }$ (B) $6 0 ^ { \\circ }$ (C) $7 0 ^ { \\circ }$ (D) $8 0 ^ { \\circ }$"
        },
